{
  "subtype": "what_if",
  "outcome_variable": "resolution_duration",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "primary_issue_category",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": true,
      "rationale": "The query targets eliminating 'the most common operational burdens'; the top issue category (database_access, email_service, etc.) operationalizes the burden being intervened on. However, an issue's intrinsic category is largely an immutable attribute of the incident rather than a settable lever, so its status as a true intervention is questionable."
    },
    {
      "name": "issue_scope",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Scope (system_wide vs individual) plausibly affects both which burdens arise and resolution duration, functioning as a common cause. Not named in the query."
    },
    {
      "name": "technical_complexity",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Complexity influences both the type/frequency of operational burden and the duration to resolve, a common cause not named in the query. Could arguably be a mediator, but as a pre-existing property it acts as a confounder."
    },
    {
      "name": "issue_recurrence_pattern",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Recurrence relates to which burdens are 'most common' and to how long resolution takes; a common cause not named in the query."
    },
    {
      "name": "assignee_resolution_efficiency",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Technician efficiency affects duration and can correlate with the burdens handled; plausible confounder not named in the query, though it borders on being a direct cause of the outcome only."
    },
    {
      "name": "assigned_technician_continuity",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Continuity of the assigned technician can affect both handling of recurring burdens and duration; plausible confounder not named in the query."
    }
  ],
  "treatment_present": true,
  "treatment_columns": ["primary_issue_category"],
  "treatment_intervenable": false,
  "confounders": [
    {"name": "issue_scope", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.6},
    {"name": "technical_complexity", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.6},
    {"name": "issue_recurrence_pattern", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.55},
    {"name": "assignee_resolution_efficiency", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.4},
    {"name": "assigned_technician_continuity", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.45}
  ],
  "confounder_quality": 0.5,
  "rationale": "The query poses a what-if: eliminating common operational burdens and measuring the drop in resolution_duration. The added set identifies the burden being intervened on (primary_issue_category) and several unnamed common causes that would need adjustment. However, the treatment is weak on intervenability: the 'burden' is captured as an intrinsic issue category/type, which an actor cannot straightforwardly SET or CHANGE per ticket — eliminating a burden class is a systemic policy, but the column encodes an immutable attribute of each incident rather than a configurable lever. Thus treatment_intervenable=false. The confounders are plausible and mostly not named in the query, showing some capability, though a few (efficiency) verge on being outcome-adjacent direct causes rather than common causes of the treatment. Overall a partial realization of the interventional claim."
}

